<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<section xml:id="sec-intro-exponential-functions" xmlns:xml="http://www.w3.org/XML/1998/namespace">
  <title>Introduction to Exponential Functions</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>An exponential function models repeated multiplication. Unlike a linear function, which changes by adding the same amount repeatedly, an exponential function changes by multiplying by the same factor repeatedly.</p>
  </introduction>

  <definition xml:id="def-exponential-function">
    <title>Exponential Function</title>
    <p>An exponential function has the form <m>f(x)=a(b)^x</m>, where <m>a\neq 0</m>, <m>b&gt;0</m>, and <m>b\neq 1</m>.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><p><m>a</m> is the initial value, or y-intercept.</p></li>
      <li><p><m>b</m> is the growth or decay factor.</p></li>
    </ul>
  </definition>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-growth-and-decay">
    <title>Growth and Decay</title>

    <definition xml:id="def-exponential-growth">
      <title>Exponential Growth</title>
      <p>If <m>b&gt;1</m>, the function represents exponential growth.</p>
    </definition>

    <definition xml:id="def-exponential-decay">
      <title>Exponential Decay</title>
      <p>If <m>0&lt;b&lt;1</m>, the function represents exponential decay.</p>
    </definition>

    <example xml:id="ex-lily-pad-growth">
      <title>Lily Pad Growth</title>
      <statement>
        <p>A lake starts with 3 lily pads and the number of lily pads doubles each day.</p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>The initial value is 3 and the growth factor is 2.</p>
        <me>L(t)=3(2)^t.</me>
      </solution>
    </example>

    <table xml:id="tbl-growth-example">
      <title>Lily Pad Growth Data</title>
      <tabular>
        <row header="yes"><cell>Day</cell><cell>0</cell><cell>1</cell><cell>2</cell><cell>3</cell><cell>4</cell></row>
        <row><cell>L(t)</cell><cell>3</cell><cell>6</cell><cell>12</cell><cell>24</cell><cell>48</cell></row>
      </tabular>
    </table>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-writing-exponential-equations">
    <title>Writing an Exponential Equation</title>

    <p>The key pieces of information are the initial value and the growth or decay factor.</p>

    <example xml:id="ex-write-growth-equation">
      <title>Writing an Equation from Context</title>
      <statement>
        <p>A population starts at 500 and increases by 8% each year. Write an exponential model.</p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>The initial value is 500.</p>
        <p>An increase of 8% corresponds to a growth factor of <m>1.08</m>.</p>
        <me>P(t)=500(1.08)^t.</me>
      </solution>
    </example>

    <example xml:id="ex-write-decay-equation">
      <title>Writing a Decay Equation</title>
      <statement>
        <p>A pond contains 400 flowers. Each day, only one-quarter of the flowers remain.</p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>The initial value is 400 and the decay factor is <m>\frac14</m>.</p>
        <me>P(t)=400\left(\frac14\right)^t.</me>
      </solution>
    </example>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-graphs-properties">
    <title>Graphs and Properties</title>

    <!-- Figure: Compare the graphs of y=3(2)^x and y=400(1/4)^x. Emphasize the common horizontal asymptote y=0 and the different growth/decay behavior. -->

    <important>
      <ul>
        <li><p>The domain of an exponential function is all real numbers.</p></li>
        <li><p>If <m>a&gt;0</m>, the range is <m>y&gt;0</m>.</p></li>
        <li><p>The graph has a horizontal asymptote at <m>y=0</m>.</p></li>
        <li><p>The y-intercept is <m>(0,a)</m>.</p></li>
      </ul>
    </important>

    <example xml:id="ex-identify-parameters">
      <title>Reading Information from an Equation</title>
      <statement>
        <p>Consider <m>C(t)=5(3)^t</m>.</p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <ul>
          <li><p>Initial value: 5</p></li>
          <li><p>Growth factor: 3</p></li>
          <li><p>y-intercept: (0,5)</p></li>
          <li><p>The function represents exponential growth.</p></li>
        </ul>
      </solution>
    </example>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsec-finding-equations-from-points">
    <title>Finding an Equation from Points</title>

    <example xml:id="ex-known-yint">
      <title>Known y-Intercept</title>
      <statement>
        <p>An exponential function passes through the points <m>(0,2)</m> and <m>(4,10)</m>. Find an equation for the function.</p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>Because the graph passes through <m>(0,2)</m>, the initial value is <m>a=2</m>.</p>
        <p>Substitute <m>(4,10)</m> into <m>f(x)=2b^x</m>.</p>
        <me>10=2b^4.</me>
        <me>b^4=5.</me>
        <me>b=\sqrt[4]{5}.</me>
        <p>Therefore,</p>
        <me>f(x)=2\left(\sqrt[4]{5}\right)^x.</me>
      </solution>
    </example>
  </subsection>
</section>
